course: dictionary
lesson: 4079
title: Germination: how a seed wakes up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A seed is a tiny package with a baby plant inside it. It does not start to grow until the conditions are right. Germination is the name for that moment when a seed starts to grow into a new plant. «A seed takes in water, swells and splits; first a root grows down, then a shoot grows up, and this is called germination.»
explain: Notice the order. The root grows down into the soil first, so the plant can drink water. Then the shoot grows up towards the light. Both are the start of a new plant, and that is why germination matters.
explain: In a plant's life cycle, germination comes after seed dispersal, when seeds are scattered away from the parent plant, and before the young plant grows bigger. A seed needs water, warmth and oxygen to germinate.
explain: Scientists test germination with fair tests. They might plant seeds in warm and cold places, or give them different amounts of water, and then compare what happens. Keep everything the same except the one thing you are testing.
fact: Germination means a seed starts to grow into a new plant.
fact: In germination, the root grows first, then the shoot.
fact: A seed needs water, warmth and oxygen to germinate.
fact: The phrase «conditions for germination» means the things a seed needs to start growing.
fact: The «germination rate» tells you how quickly, or how many, seeds start to grow in a test.
exercise: «A seed takes in water, swells and splits; first a root grows down, then a shoot grows up, and this is called germination.» What is germination? | The soil a plant grows in | *The process where a seed starts to grow into a new plant | The way seeds are carried away from the parent plant | The flower that opens in spring
exercise: In germination, which part grows first? | A flower | A leaf | *A root | A shoot
exercise: What do seeds need to start germinating? | Sweets and sugar | Sand only | *Water, warmth and oxygen | Bright paint
test: Mia puts seeds in a warm place and in a cold place to see which ones germinate. What kind of test is this? | A seed dispersal | A shoot test | A guess | *A fair test
test: Which phrase means how quickly or how many seeds start to grow in a test? | Conditions for germination | Root hair | Seed dispersal | *Germination rate
exam: In the life cycle of a plant, which comes first: germination or seed dispersal? | Germination | *Seed dispersal | Neither happens | Both at the same time
